{
  "id": "Compound::DB00091 - Gene::1551",
  "verbalization": "Cyclosporine is implicated in the development of Uraemic Syndrome, which is caused by Quinine or Quinidine, both of which bind to Cyp3a7. The lowest common ancestor of these relationships is the Organonitrogen Compound class, suggesting a shared mechanism of action. The evidence indicates that Cyclosporine may influence the expression or activity of Cyp3a7 through the induction of Uraemic Syndrome, which in turn is associated with the binding of quinones to the enzyme. This pathway highlights a potential link between Cyclosporine and Cyp3a7, mediated through the metabolic effects of quinones. The integration of these relationships underscores a possible role of Cyclosporine in modulating Cyp3a7 activity via the indirect pathway of Uraemic Syndrome.",
